          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            exists without reference to any predefined notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+fields"} quantum fields
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            etc.:
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            in
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiomatization"} axiomatization
            is simply a consistent assignment of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+quantum+states"} spaces of quantum states
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiomatization"} axiomatization
            it is a consistent assignment of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+quantum+observables"} algebras of quantum observables
          %p
            However, most (or maybe all?) quantum field theories of interest in actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            (as opposed to as devices of pure
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            from a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian"}> local
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+Lagrangian"}> extended
            )
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+fields"} quantum fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+duals"} holographic duals
            of quantum field theories that arise by quantization. Moreover, the extra information provided by the Lagrangian is commonly used (and is maybe strictly necessary) to interpret the mathematical structure of the axiomatic QFT in actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            (though notably in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            there are results that re-extract at least parts of this data from the axiomatic QFT, for instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Doplicher-Roberts+reconstruction+theorem"} Doplicher-Roberts reconstruction theorem
            which extract the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+gauge+group"} global gauge group
            from the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net+of+quantum+observables"} local net of quantum observables
          %p
            There are in turn two formalizations of the notion of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            %em
              algebraic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
            when referring to a precursor step to the actual quantization step, in which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            is lifted to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"} prequantum bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometric+quantization"} higher geometric quantization
            this prequantization step is already part of the data of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            itself: an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+Lagrangian"} extended Lagrangian
            already encodes not just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            but also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+bundle"} prequantum bundle
            and all the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+n-bundle"} prequantum (n-k)-bundles
            in each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+0-bundle"} prequantum 0-bundle
            in this context.
          %p
            Therefore, in the refined picture of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+quantum+field+theory"} extended quantum field theory
            it makes good sense to refer in a unified way to
            %strong prequantum field theory
            for all of the data related to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangians"} Lagrangians
            that is not yet the final
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %p
            In particular, an
            %strong extended prequantum field theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is a rule that assigns
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                to every (suitably
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed manifold
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+n-bundle"} prequantum (n-k)-bundle
            %li
              %p
                to every (suitably
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} oriented
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+with+boundary"} manifold with boundary
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_k
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
                of the prequantum
                = succeed "-bundle" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mi k
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-k+1)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial \Sigma_k
                and pulled back to the space of fields over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_k
          %p
            such that this data is related suitably under
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression"} transgression
          %p
            The actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+quantum+field+theory"} extended quantum field theory
            would be obtained from such a data by passing from the assignment of a given prequantum
            = succeed "-bundle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-k)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-vector+space"} (n-k)-vector space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            of a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+infinity-bundle"} associated
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+infinity-bundle"} fiber bundle
          %p This is summarized in the following table:
          %div
            %p
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+prequantum+field+theory"} extended prequantum field theory
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi k
                          %mo ≤
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq k \leq n
                  %th
                    (off-shell)
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+n-bundle"} prequantum (n-k)-bundle
                  %th traditional terminology
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+characteristic+map"} universal characteristic map
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level+%28Chern-Simons+theory%29"} level
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
