                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}^n U(1)
            (the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-group"} circle (n+1)-group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-functor"} monoidal (∞,n)-functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi n
                    %mo ⊗
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Span
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi n
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S \colon Bord_n^\otimes \to Span_n(\mathbf{H}, \mathbf{B}^n U(1))
                  \\,.
          %p
            The point of local topological (prequantum) field theory is that by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+theorem"} cobordism theorem
            the above story reverses: the assignment of fields and their action functional in higher dimension is necessarily given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+traces"} higher traces
            of the data assigned in lower dimension. Hence the whole assignment
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            above is fixed by its value on the point, hence by a choice of one single map
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi Fields
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo *
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
