                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_k
          %p (…)
          %h4#BoundaryFieldTheory Boundary field theory
          %p (…)
          %h4#CornerFieldTheory Corner field theory
          %p (…)
          %h3#classical_mechanics_as_prequantum_field_theory Classical mechanics as prequantum field theory
          %p
            Traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+mechanics"}> Hamiltonian mechanics
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+mechanics"} Lagrangian mechanics
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton-Jacobi+theory"} Hamilton-Jacobi theory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi conn
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantized+Lagrangian+correspondence"} prequantized Lagrangian correspondence
              \.
          %h3#HigherDijkgraafWittenLocalPrequantumFieldTheory Higher Dijkgraaf-Witten local prequantum field theory
          %p
            We discuss here aspects of higher
            = succeed "-type" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Fields
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Fields}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} discrete ∞-groupoid
            (and usually also required to be finite, especially if its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            is considered). This is a special case of the higher Chern-Simons theories discussed below in
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#HigherChern-SimonsLocalPrequantumFieldTheory"} Higher Chern-Simons local prequantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            by way of pertinent examples.
          %p
            The original
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
            is that in dimension 3 (reviewed in
            %em
              %a{:href => "#3dDWLocalPrequantumFieldTheory"} 3d Local prequantum field theory
            below), which was introduced in (
            %a{:href => "Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory#DijkgraafWitten90"}> Dijkgraaf-Witten 90
            ) as a toy version of standard 3d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply+connected+topological+space"} simply connected
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory#FreedQuinn93"} Freed-Quinn 93
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory#Freed94"} Freed 94
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#FHLT"} FHLT 10
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LocalPrequantumFieldWithAction"}
            is sketched for Dijkgraaf-Witten type theories, and in section 8 there the quantization of these theories to genuine local quantum field theories is sketched.
          %h4#1dDWTheory 1d Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            1 is what results when one regards a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+character"} group character
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            as a local
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            in
          %p
            the sense of def.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LocalPrequantumFieldWithAction"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+1-types"}> homotopy 1-types
            ).
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#FiniteGaugeGroups"} Finite gauge groups
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#GroupoidsAndBasicHomotopy1TypeTheory"} Essence of gauge theory: Groupoids and basic homotopy 1-type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#CorrespondencesOfGroupoids"} Trajectories of fields: Correspondences of groupoids
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#1dDWLocalFieldTheory"} Action functionals on spaces of trajectories: Correspondences of groupoids over the space of phases
          %p
            The punchline of this section is little theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GroupCharacterPrequantumTheoryOnCircle"}
            at the very end, which states that the 1d local prequantum field theory whose local action functional is the delooping of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+character"} group character
            assigns to the circle the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            which is again that group character. The proof of this statement is an unwinding of the basic mechanisms of local prequantum field theories.
          %h5#FiniteGaugeGroups Finite gauge groups
          %p First some brief remarks, before we dive into the formalism.
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+character"} group character
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \to U(1)
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            (taken here as a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete group
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            of a physical field theory. The simplest such case is a field theory such that on the point there is just a single possible field configuration, to be denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            may think of the fields as being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connections"} connections
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+potentials"}> vector potentials
            ”), hence represented by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+1-forms"} differential 1-forms
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0
          %p
            Even though there is just a single such field, that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            means that for each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/element"} element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in G
            there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
            that takes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0
            to itself, a state of affairs which we suggestively denote by the symbols
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi_0 \stackrel{g}{\to}  \phi_0
                  \\,.
          %p
            Again, the reader familiar with traditional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            may think of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
            as in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            itself, taking the trivial field configuration to itself.
          %p
            That the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            is indeed a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            means that gauge transformations can be applied consecutively, which we express in symbols as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mn 0
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi g
                                  %mn 1
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi g
                                  %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mn 0
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi g
                                %mn 2
                              %mo ⋅
                              %msub
                                %mi g
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \phi_0
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{g_1}}\nearrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{g_2}}
                  \\\
                  \\phi_0 &amp;&amp; \underset{g_2 \cdot g_1}{\to} &amp;&amp; \phi_0
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Regarded this way, we say the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            acting on the single field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0
            forms a
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0
            and whose set of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Of course in richer field theories there may be more than one field configuration, clearly, with gauge transformations between them. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_0
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_1
            are two field configurations and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            is a gauge transformation taking one to the other, we may usefully denote this by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mn 0
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi g
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\phi_0 &amp;\stackrel{g_1}{\to}&amp; \phi_1
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p Similarly then for yet another gauge configuration to another field configuration
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi g
                                %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\phi_1 &amp;\stackrel{g_2}{\to}&amp; \phi_2
                  }
          %p then composing them gives the picture
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi g
                                  %mn 1
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo ↘
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi g
                                  %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mn 0
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi g
                                %mn 2
                              %mo ⋅
                              %msub
                                %mi g
                                %mn 1
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; \phi_1
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{g_1}}\nearrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{g_2}}
                  \\\
                  \\phi_0 &amp;&amp; \underset{g_2 \cdot g_1}{\to} &amp;&amp; \phi_2
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            We now discuss this notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            more formally.
          %h5#GroupoidsAndBasicHomotopy1TypeTheory Essence of gauge theory: Groupoids and basic homotopy 1-type theory
          %p
            The following is a quick review of basics of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            and their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+1-type"}> homotopy 1-type
            \-theory), geared towards the constructions and fact needed for 1-dimensional Dijkgraaf-Witten theory. For more along these lines see also at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+homotopy+types"} geometry of physics – homotopy types
          #Groupoid.num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              A (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"}> small
              )
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet
              is
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  a pair of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mn 0
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_0 \in Set
                  (the set of
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_1 \in Set
                  (the set of
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              %li
                %p
                  equipped with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi 𝒢
                                  %mn 1
                                %msub
                                  %mo ×
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi 𝒢
                                      %mn 0
                                %msub
                                  %mi 𝒢
                                  %mn 1
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mo ⟶
                                  %mo ∘
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi 𝒢
                                  %mn 1
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mover
                                    %munder
                                      %mo ⟶
                                      %mi s
                                    %mover
                                      %mo ←
                                      %mi i
                                  %mover
                                    %mo ⟶
                                    %mi t
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mi 𝒢
                                  %mn 0
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ,
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\array{
                        \\mathcal{G}_1 \times_{\mathcal{G}_0} \mathcal{G}_1
                        \&amp;\stackrel{\circ}{\longrightarrow}&amp;
                        \\mathcal{G}_1
                        \&amp; \stackrel{\overset{t}{\longrightarrow}}{\stackrel{\overset{i}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{s}{\longrightarrow}}}&amp;
                        \\mathcal{G}_0
                        }\,,
                %p
                  where the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+product"} fiber product
                  on the left is that over
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝒢
                            %mn 1
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi t
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝒢
                            %mn 0
                          %mover
                            %mo ←
                            %mi s
                          %msub
                            %mi 𝒢
                            %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_1 \stackrel{t}{\to} \mathcal{G}_0 \stackrel{s}{\leftarrow} \mathcal{G}_1
            %p such that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                  takes values in
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphisms"} endomorphisms
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mi s
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi id
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi 𝒢
                              %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        t \circ i = s \circ i =   id_{\mathcal{G}_0}, \;\;\;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∘
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
                  defines a partial
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
                  operation which is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associative
                  and
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitality"} unital
                  for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i(\mathcal{G}_0)
                  the
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identities"} identities
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi g
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s (g \circ f) = s(f)
                  and
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi g
                          %mo ∘
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %mi t
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi g
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t (g \circ f) = t(g)
              %li
                %p
                  every morphism has an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
                  under this composition.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_7 Remark
            %p This data is visualized as follows. The set of morphisms is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mn 0
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi k
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{G}_1
                    \=
                    \\left\{
                    \\phi_0 \stackrel{k}{\to} \phi_1
                    \\right\}
            %p and the set of pairs of composable morphisms is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ≔
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 1
                    %munder
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mn 0
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo {
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi ϕ
                                %mn 1
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mrow
                                %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi k
                                      %mn 1
                              %mo ↗
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo ↘
                                %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                  %mrow
                                    %msub
                                      %mi k
                                      %mn 2
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi ϕ
                                %mn 0
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi k
                                    %mn 2
                                  %mo ∘
                                  %msub
                                    %mi k
                                    %mn 1
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mi ϕ
                                %mn 2
                      %mo }
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathcal{G}_2 \coloneqq \mathcal{G}_1 \underset{\mathcal{G}_0}{\times} \mathcal{G}_1
                    \=
                    \\left\{
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; \phi_1
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{k_1}}\nearrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{k_2}}
                    \\\
                    \\phi_0 &amp;&amp; \stackrel{k_2 \circ k_1}{\to} &amp;&amp; \phi_2
                    }
                    \\right\}
                    \\,.
            %p
              The functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∘
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 2
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1, p_2, \circ \colon \mathcal{G}_2 \to \mathcal{G}_1
              are those which send, respectively, these triangular diagrams to the left morphism, or the right morphism, or the bottom morphism.
          #SetAsGroupoid.num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to be the set of objects and adding only precisely the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity"} identity
              morphism from each object to itself
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mover
                          %munder
                            %mo ⟶
                            %mi id
                          %mover
                            %mo ⟵
                            %mi id
                        %mover
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi id
                      %mi X
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left(
                    X
                    \\stackrel
                    {\overset{id}{\longrightarrow}}
                    {
                    \\stackrel{\overset{id}{\longleftarrow}}{\underset{id}{\longrightarrow}}
                    }
                    X
                    \\right)
                    \\,.
          #DeloopingGroupoid.num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf{B}G)_\bullet
              has
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi G
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mn 0
                        %mo =
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf{B}G)_0 = \ast
                  ;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi G
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mn 1
                        %mo =
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbf{B}G)_1 = G
                  \.
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              two groups, group homomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon G \to K
              are in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+bijection"} natural bijection
              with groupoid homomorphisms
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi f
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi K
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\mathbf{B}f)_\bullet
                    \\;\colon\;
                    (\mathbf{B}G)_\bullet \to (\mathbf{B}K)_\bullet
                    \\,.
            %p
              In particular a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+character"} group character
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \colon G \to U(1)
              is equivalently a groupoid homomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi c
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\mathbf{B}c)_\bullet
                    \\;\colon\;
                    (\mathbf{B}G)_\bullet
                    \\to
                    (\mathbf{B}U(1))_\bullet
                    \\,.
            %p
              Here, for the time being, all groups are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+groups"} discrete groups
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
              also has a standard structure of a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ♭
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Grp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\flat U(1) \in Grp
            %p
              to mean explicitly the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete group
              underlying the circle group. (Here “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ♭
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat
              ” denotes the “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+modality"}> flat modality
              ”.)
          #ActionGroupoid.num_example
            %h6#example_4 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete group
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho \colon X \times G \to X
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              (a
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation+representation"} permutation representation
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
              or
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"} homotopy quotient
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              is the groupoid
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi ρ
                    %mi G
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi G
                      %mover
                        %munder
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi p
                              %mn 1
                        %mover
                          %mo ⟶
                          %mi ρ
                      %mi X
                      %mo )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X//_\rho G
                    \=
                    \\left(
                    X \times G
                    \\stackrel{\overset{\rho}{\longrightarrow}}{\underset{p_1}{\longrightarrow}}
                    X
                    \\right)
            %p
              with composition induced by the product in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              are of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    x_1 \stackrel{g}{\to} x_2 = \rho(x_1)(g)
            %p
              for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, x_2 \in X
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \in G
          %p As an important special case we have:
          #BGGroupoidAsActionGroupoid.num_example
            %h6#example_5 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete"} discrete
              group and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
              the trivial action of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              on the point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              (the singleton set), the coresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
              according to def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ActionGroupoid"}
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
              groupoid of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              according to def.
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DeloopingGroupoid"}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\ast //G)_\bullet = (\mathbf{B}G)_\bullet
                    \\,.
            %p
              Another canonical action is the action of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              on itself by right multiplication. The corresponding action groupoid we write
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                    %mi G
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi G
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\mathbf{E}G)_\bullet \coloneqq G//G
                    \\,.
            %p
              The constant map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \to \ast
              induces a canonical morphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi G
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi G
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≃
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi E
                            %mi G
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo *
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                            %mi G
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≃
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi G
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    G//G &amp; \simeq &amp; \mathbf{E}G
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    \\ast //G &amp; \simeq &amp; \mathbf{B}G
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              This is known as the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+principal+bundle"} universal principal bundle
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PullbackOfEGGroupoidAsHomotopyFiberProduct"}
              for more on this.
          .num_example
            %h6#example_6 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              is the groupoid with
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi I
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_0 = \{a,b\}
                ;
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi I
                        %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                        %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                        %mi b
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo →
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I_1 = \{\mathrm{id}_a, \mathrm{id}_b, a \to  b \}
                \.
          #FundamentalGroupoid.num_example
            %h6#example_7 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(\Sigma)
              is
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(\Sigma)_0 =
                points in
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Σ
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mn 1
                      %mo =
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(\Sigma)_1 =
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous
                paths in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                modulo
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
                that leaves the endpoints fixed.
          #PathSpaceGroupoid.num_example
            %h6#example_8 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet
              any groupoid, there is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+space"} path space
              groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi 𝒢
                      %mo •
                      %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}^I_\bullet
              with
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mn 0
                          %mi I
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mn 1
                        %mo =
                        %mrow
                          %mo {
                          %mrow
                            %mtable
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                  %msub
                                    %mi ϕ
                                    %mn 0
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                  %msup
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                    %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                      %mi k
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                  %msub
                                    %mi ϕ
                                    %mn 1
                          %mo }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}^I_0 = \mathcal{G}_1 = \left\{ \array{ \phi_0 \\ \downarrow^{\mathrlap{k}} \\ \phi_1  } \right\}
                  ;
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mn 1
                          %mi I
                        %mo =
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}^I_1 =
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting squares
                  in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒢
                          %mo •
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}_\bullet
                  \=
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mo {
                          %mrow
                            %mtable
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                  %msub
                                    %mi ϕ
                                    %mn 0
                                %mtd
                                  %mover
                                    %mo →
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mi h
                                        %mn 0
                                %mtd
                                  %msub
                                    %mover
                                      %mi ϕ
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                                    %mn 0
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                  %msup
                                    %mrow
                                    %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                      %mi k
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                %mtd
                                %mtd
                                  %msup
                                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                                    %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                      %mover
                                        %mi k
                                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                              %mtr
                                %mtd
                                  %msub
                                    %mi ϕ
                                    %mn 1
                                %mtd
                                  %mover
                                    %mo →
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mi h
                                        %mn 1
                                %mtd
                                  %msub
                                    %mover
                                      %mi ϕ
                                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                                    %mn 1
                          %mo }
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo .
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\left\{
                        \\array{
                        \\phi_0 &amp;\stackrel{h_0}{\to}&amp; \tilde \phi_0
                        \\\
                        {}^{\mathllap{k}}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{\tilde k}}
                        \\\
                        \\phi_1 &amp;\stackrel{h_1}{\to}&amp; \tilde \phi_1
                        }
                        \\right\}
                        \\,.
            %p This comes with two canonical homomorphisms
            .maruku-equation
