                    Universal boundary condition for
                    = succeed ":" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi S
                              %mi tYM
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{tYM}
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#GenralBoundaryForStYMAndHigherChernSimons"}
                  General boundary conditions: Higher Chern-Weil theory and
                  = succeed "-Chern-Simons" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            where we discuss how the boundary theories for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi tYM
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{n+1}_{tYM}
            are precisely the prequantum field theories of higher
            = succeed "-type," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Simons+theories"} ∞-Chern-Simons theories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            on ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
            but also higher Chern-Simons theory on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+fields"} higher gauge fields
            such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/String+2-group"} String 2-group
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7-dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} 7-dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AKSZ+sigma-models"} AKSZ sigma-models
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+string+field+theory"} closed string field theory
          %p
            This shows how
            = succeed "-Chern-Simons" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theories"} boundary field theories
            of the canonical local field theory which exists in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesive
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
          %p
            Continuing in this vein we can then work out what all the further higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theories"} boundary field theories
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defect+field+theories"} defect field theories
            of this universal higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+Yang-Mills+theory"} topological Yang-Mills theory
            and hence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/%E2%88%9E-Chern-Simons+theories"} ∞-Chern-Simons theories
            are. We find
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => ""} Topological Chern-Simons boundaries
          %p
            which are given by generalized “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bohr-Sommerfeld+leaf"} Bohr-Sommerfeld
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotropic+subspaces"} isotropic subspaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks"} moduli stacks
            of
            = succeed "-Chern-Simons" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p Then…
          %p (…)
          %h4#TopologicalYangMillsLocalPrequantumFieldTheory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = n + 1
            , Universal topological Yang-Mills theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi tYM
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{tYM}
          %p
            There is a special and especially simple map to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficient"} coefficient
            object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                  %msup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat \mathbf{B}^{n+1} U(1)
            for flat
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+action+functionals"} local action functionals
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+n-bundles"} prequantum n-bundles
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msubsup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mi cl
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %mi exp
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi i
                                %msub
                                  %mi S
                                  %mi tYM
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mrow
                              %mi n
                              %mo +
                              %mn 1
                          %mo ♭
                          %mi U
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\Omega^{n+1}_{cl}
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\exp(i S_{tYM})}}
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{B}^{n+1} \flat U(1)
                  }
          %p
            which in components is the inclusion of closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypercohomology"} hypercohomology
            cocoycles.
          %p
            We here introduce and describe this map and then regard it as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+action+functional"} local action functional
            of a local prequantum field theory according to def.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LocalPrequantumFieldWithAction"}
            %em
              %a{:href => "#HigherChernSimonsPrequantumFieldTheory"} Higher Chern-Simons prequantum field theory
            we find that this field theory is such that close to its boundaries it looks like (higher)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+Yang-Mills+theory"} topological Yang-Mills theory
            for every possible higher gauge group and every possible
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            on it, as one considers every possible
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+condition"} boundary condition
          %h5#smooth_moduli_stacks_of_fields_smooth_groupoids
            Smooth moduli stacks of fields: Smooth
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            The notion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf"} sheaf
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
            or equivalently of a chain complex of sheaves over a fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            has a long tradition in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            in any degree may be “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+a+differential+form"}> pulled back
            ” along any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
            between
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SmoothMfd"} SmoothMfd
            of smooth manifolds as a replacement for and generalization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+open+subsets"} category of open subsets
            of any given one, then differential forms constitute a sheaf on that
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi SmoothMfd
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega^{n} \colon SmoothMfd^{op} \to Set
                  \\,.
          %p
            In particular for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0
            this is just the sheaf of smooth functions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ̲
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi SmoothMfd
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\underline{\mathbb{R}} = C^\infty(-,\mathbb{R}) \colon SmoothMfd^{op} \to Set
                  \\,.
          %p
            One way to think of this is that this sheaf
            %em is
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            first of all as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            – which is the value of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ̲
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{\mathbb{R}}
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point"} point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            – and secondly equipped with its canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
            which is encoded by the system of
            %em all
            sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X,\mathbb{R})
            of smooth functions from any smooth manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(\phi, \mathbb{R}) \colon C^\infty(Y, \mathbb{R}) \to C^\infty(X,\mathbb{R})
            of how these functions pull back (precompose) along any smooth function of test manifolds
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon X \to Y
          %p
            Regarding the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            this way means regarding it as what is sometimes called a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+space"} diffeological space
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+space"} smooth space
          %p
            Therefore we will often just write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            when we really mean the sheaf
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ̲
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underline{\mathbb{R}}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} represented
            by it.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan map
            of def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DoldKanMap"}
